mqnic: rx: drop dma_sync_single_range_for_cpu() after dma_unmap_page()

mqnic_process_rx_cq() unmaps the receive page, clears rx_info->dma_addr,
and then calls dma_sync_single_range_for_cpu() with that cleared handle.
The unmap already hands the buffer back to the CPU, and a sync on a
handle that is no longer mapped has no valid meaning. On a
cache-coherent host (x86 PCIe) it is a no-op; on a non-coherent platform
such as a Zynq UltraScale+ without dma-coherent it adds a cache
maintenance operation on every received packet, on DMA address 0.

Against Corundum commit 1ca0151 (modules/mqnic/mqnic_rx.c).
Apply from the Corundum root:  patch -p1 < 0001-mqnic-rx-drop-sync-after-unmap.patch

diff --git a/modules/mqnic/mqnic_rx.c b/modules/mqnic/mqnic_rx.c
index dfd479c3..17031144 100644
--- a/modules/mqnic/mqnic_rx.c
+++ b/modules/mqnic/mqnic_rx.c
@@ -367,9 +367,6 @@ int mqnic_process_rx_cq(struct mqnic_cq *cq, int napi_budget)
 				dma_unmap_len(rx_info, len), DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 		rx_info->dma_addr = 0;
 
-		dma_sync_single_range_for_cpu(dev, rx_info->dma_addr, rx_info->page_offset,
-				rx_info->len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
-
 		__skb_fill_page_desc(skb, 0, page, rx_info->page_offset, len);
 		rx_info->page = NULL;
 
